================
Multicast Storm Control

Why we did this
================
Broadcast Storm Control protects against excessive Broadcast traffic.

However, excessive Multicast traffic can also impact network performance.

Common sources include:

- IPTV
- Video Streaming
- Live Broadcasting
- Misconfigured Applications
- Multicast Flooding Attacks

When Multicast traffic becomes excessive:

- Network bandwidth is consumed
- Switch CPU utilization increases
- End-user performance degrades
- Legitimate applications are affected

To prevent this, Cisco introduced Multicast Storm Control.

Multicast Storm Control limits the amount of multicast traffic allowed on a switch port.

What will happen
================
1. Switch monitors multicast traffic.
2. A multicast threshold is configured.
3. Traffic below the threshold is forwarded normally.
4. Traffic above the threshold is dropped.
5. Excessive multicast traffic is contained.
6. Network resources remain protected.

Example

Normal Operation
----------------

Multicast Traffic:
5%

Configured Threshold:
10%

Result:
--------
Traffic Allowed


Multicast Storm
---------------

Multicast Traffic:
35%

Configured Threshold:
10%

Result:
--------
Excess Traffic Dropped

Network Protected

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Protects against multicast flooding.
2. Preserves available bandwidth.
3. Reduces switch CPU load.
4. Improves network stability.
5. Prevents multicast traffic abuse.
6. Easy to implement.
7. Protects critical applications.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Incorrect thresholds may impact valid multicast applications.
2. Requires network traffic analysis.
3. Does not control broadcast storms.
4. Does not control unknown unicast storms.
5. Does not remove the source of the storm.

Hence what came next
--------------------
After controlling Broadcast and Multicast traffic, administrators realized that Unknown Unicast traffic could also consume excessive bandwidth.

Unknown Unicast traffic occurs when the destination MAC address is not found in the CAM table.

Large amounts of Unknown Unicast traffic can behave similarly to a flooding attack.

This led to:

Unknown Unicast Storm Control

which limits excessive unknown unicast traffic before it affects the network.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Configure Multicast Storm Control

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# storm-control multicast level 10.00


(Optional)

Switch(config-if)# storm-control action trap


(Optional)

Switch(config-if)# storm-control action shutdown

Verification
============

show storm-control

show storm-control interface fa0/1

show running-config interface fa0/1

Expected Result
===============

Normal Multicast Traffic:
-------------------------
Allowed

Traffic Below Threshold:
------------------------
Forwarded

Traffic Above Threshold:
------------------------
Dropped

Network Stability:
------------------
Maintained

Multicast Storm:
----------------
Contained

Summary
=======

Problem:
Excessive multicast traffic can consume bandwidth and impact network performance.

Solution:
Multicast Storm Control limits multicast traffic on switch ports.

Benefit:
Protects network resources and improves stability.

Limitation:
Does not protect against Unknown Unicast flooding.

